# Stack profile — the only place that knows how this project builds and tests.
# Flat `key: value` pairs. A command that is not declared here is skipped by the
# gate and named in its report; it never fails a stage. Every value is run through a
# shell, so a stack that needs two commands chains them (`a && b`) — except the command
# the gate appends a single-test selector to (`e2eTest:`, or `test:` when there is no
# `e2eTest:`), which stays one command, because the selector would reach only the last.
#
# compile      makes the test sources compile without running them
# test         runs the unit tests
# e2eTest      runs the end-user tests (falls back to `test` when absent)
# filterFlag   the flag that selects a single test
# filterFormat how a selector `<Class>#<method>` is written for this runner
# testReport  where this runner leaves its report of what it executed, as a glob. Found by
#              convention when absent: JUnit XML (`build/test-results/**`, `surefire-reports/*`)
#              and TRX (`TestResults/**`). Any stack can emit one of those two — pytest with
#              `--junitxml`, jest with jest-junit, `gotestsum --junitfile`, `cargo nextest`,
#              PHPUnit, RSpec, the .NET platform with `--logger trx` — so naming the path is all
#              a new stack needs
# testEvidence `exit-code` to accept the weaker check where a runner writes no report at all. The
#              gate then says so on every single test, because an exit code cannot distinguish a
#              failing test from a runner that never ran one
# covers.<key> which tests a command runs, where its own text does not say: `**` for every
#              test in the project (`dotnet test` on a solution), or a comma-separated list
#              of path prefixes. Needed because whether a pathless command runs everything
#              or one source set is knowledge about that build tool — the gate refuses to
#              guess it, and running the wrong task would call a criterion covered that
#              nothing executes
# architecture runs the architecture rule suite — the gate runs it in the build stage
# format       runs the formatter check — the gate runs it in the build stage
# carrier.<s>  the skill or agent that carries one stage's craft (`carrier.test`, `carrier.build`,
#              `carrier.plan`). A skill works in every tool; an agent only where a tool has them
# knowledge    the ONE skill a stage may treat as an authority, with citations. Name it only when
#              the project vouches for it being current: a vendored catalog from an older release
#              names rules and markers that no longer exist, and a citation makes that look verified.
#              Unnamed means the stages work from the project's own rules, markers and documents
# contextMap   where the project keeps its context map (found by convention when absent)
# glossary     file name of a bounded context's glossary (default: glossary.md anywhere)
# reviews      perspectives the judge stage covers *in addition to* domain, boundaries and craft
# review.<p>   the reviewer that should carry one perspective. A review *skill* works in every
#              tool; a review *agent* only in a tool that has agents — where it is missing, the
#              judge runs the built-in pass itself and says so

compile: {{COMPILE_COMMAND}}
test: {{TEST_COMMAND}}
e2eTest: {{E2E_TEST_COMMAND}}
filterFlag: {{FILTER_FLAG}}
filterFormat: {{FILTER_FORMAT}}
architecture: {{ARCHITECTURE_COMMAND}}
format: {{FORMAT_COMMAND}}
#carrier.test: <the skill that carries this project's end-user testing craft>
#carrier.build: <the skill that carries this project's implementation craft>
# A perspective is found by description when a matching review skill is installed; name a carrier
# here only to override that choice or to bind an added perspective.
#reviews: security, performance
#review.security: <the review skill that carries it>
